  TELUGU. The demo language. Every key in values/strings.xml belongs here in Telugu.

  RULES, and they are not negotiable (docs/decisions/0017):
  1. A Telugu string is written or reviewed by a FLUENT SPEAKER before it ships. Vedant does not
     read Telugu well enough to catch a wrong word, and a wrong word on a health screen is read by
     a judge who does.
  2. No machine translation, no guessed text, no placeholder. A key that is not here falls back to
     the default table at runtime, visibly. That is the honest state of an untranslated string.
  3. An entry that has been written but NOT yet reviewed carries the comment REVIEW on the line
     above it, with the author's name. StringResourcesTest lists every unreviewed and every
     missing key in its output, so the review queue is a test report rather than a memory.
  4. Meaning follows spec 15.2: what the data says, what someone might consider, the decision
     handed back. Numbers come from the database. A translation that adds a claim is wrong even
     if the Telugu is perfect.

